# Lexical Structure & Token Physics
File: openOODA/docs/spec/01_lexical.oot

This module defines the lexical physics, token inventory, character encoding,
literals, and serialization protocols of the openOODA compiler ecosystem.

## 1. Character Encoding & Source Physics

1. UTF-8 Encoding: All openOODA source files (.oo) and governance files (.oot)
   must be valid UTF-8 sequences.
2. Byte Order Mark (BOM): A Unicode Byte Order Mark (U+FEFF) is allowed ONLY at
   byte offset 0 of a file. A BOM at any other offset is an error (ERR\tlex).
3. Whitespace: Whitespace includes Space (0x20), Horizontal Tab (0x09), Line
   Feed (0x0A), and Carriage Return (0x0D). Whitespace separates tokens.
4. Comments: Line comments start with "//" and continue to the end of the line.
   Block comments ("/* ... */") are strictly prohibited.
5. Line Limit: Every source and governance file must contain <= 256 lines.

## 2. Academy 4-Element Header Grammar

Every production source file (.oo) must begin with an Academy header:
1. Title: Line 1 must match "// # <Title>".
2. Logline: Must match "// Logline: <Sentence>." (ends with a period).
3. Setup: Must match "// Setup: <Preconditions>.".
4. Beats: Must match "// Beats:\n//   1. <Beat 1>\n//   2. <Beat 2>...".

## 3. Identifiers

Identifiers name functions, variables, types, and modules:
- Syntax: [a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*
- Case sensitivity: Identifiers are case-sensitive.
- Filename law: Canonical file names must be strictly lowercase (RULES §1.19).

## 4. Keyword Partitioning

### 4.1 Active Normative Keywords (22)
The active compiler and runtime recognize exactly 22 keywords:
- Control Flow: if, else, match, for, in, while, return, break, continue.
- Declarations: fn, let, pub, mut, import, type, export.
- Contracts: requires, ensures, verify.
- Execution: gpu.
- Literals: true, false.

### 4.2 Residual Non-Normative Keywords (5)
The following 5 keywords are reserved for architectural vision:
trait, newtype, where, spec, impl.
The compiler parser rejects these keywords. Language servers suppress them.

## 5. Literals & Value Representation

1. Integer Literals:
   - Decimal: 0 or [1-9][0-9]*
   - Hexadecimal: 0x[0-9a-fA-F]+
   - Binary: 0b[01]+
   - Octal: 0o[0-7]+
   - Range: Signed 64-bit integer (-9223372036854775808 to 9223372036854775807).
2. Float Literals:
   - Decimal float: [0-9]+\.[0-9]+([eE][+-]?[0-9]+)?
   - Invariant: Floats require a leading integer digit ("0.5", not ".5").
   - Precision: 64-bit IEEE 754 double precision.
3. String Literals:
   - Delimited by double quotes: "...".
   - Escape sequences: \n (LF), \t (tab), \r (CR), \0 (NUL), \" (quote), \\.
   - Unknown escape sequences preserve character verbatim without backslash.
4. Boolean Literals: true and false.
5. Unit Literal: () represents the empty tuple.

## 6. Complete Token Inventory (oodac/types/token_typed.oo)

The typed token table defines 48 mapped tokens and integer IDs (0..99):

### 6.1 Primitive & Source Tokens
- IDENT (0): User identifier.
- INT (1): Integer literal.
- FLOAT (2): Floating-point literal.
- STRING (3): String literal.
- LINEFILE (4): Source location line and file directive.
- SRC_PATH (5): Source file path directive.
- EOF (99): End of token stream.

### 6.2 Keyword Tokens (KW_*)
- KW_FN (10): fn
- KW_LET (11): let
- KW_PUB (12): pub
- KW_MUT (13): mut
- KW_IMPORT (14): import
- KW_REQUIRES (15): requires
- KW_ENSURES (16): ensures
- KW_VERIFY (17): verify
- KW_IF (18): if
- KW_ELSE (19): else
- KW_MATCH (20): match
- KW_FOR (21): for
- KW_IN (22): in
- KW_WHILE (23): while
- KW_RETURN (24): return
- KW_TYPE (25): type
- KW_WHERE (26): where (residual)
- KW_TRUE (27): true
- KW_FALSE (28): false
- KW_GPU (29): gpu
- KW_BREAK (30): break
- KW_CONTINUE (31): continue
- KW_TRAIT (32): trait (residual)
- KW_IMPL (33): impl (residual)
- KW_EXPORT (34): export

### 6.3 Operator Tokens
- SLASH (40): /
- PLUS (41): +
- MINUS (42): -
- STAR (43): *
- PERCENT (44): %
- EQEQ (45): ==
- FATARROW (46): =>
- EQ (47): =
- NEQ (48): !=
- LSHIFT (49): <<
- RSHIFT (50): >>
- LTE (51): <=
- LT (52): <
- GTE (53): >=
- GT (54): >
- ANDAND (55): &&
- AMP (56): &
- OROR (57): ||
- PIPE (58): |
- CARET (59): ^
- BANG (74): !

### 6.4 Delimiter & Punctuation Tokens
- COLON (60): :
- SEMI (61): ;
- COMMA (62): ,
- DOTDOTEQ (63): ..=
- DOTDOT (64): ..
- DOT (65): .
- LPAREN (66): (
- RPAREN (67): )
- LBRACE (68): {
- RBRACE (69): }
- LBRACKET (70): [
- RBRACKET (71): ]
- QUESTION (72): ?
- AT (73): @

Note: The raw scanner also recognizes return arrow "->" (ARROW).

## 7. Machine Wire Token Protocol

The compiler emits tab-separated token streams via "oodac tokens <file.oo>":
<KIND>\t<LINE>\t<COL>\t<TEXT>
Every stream terminates with:
EOF\t<line>\t<col>\t

Escaping rules for TSV:
- Tab is escaped as \t.
- Newline is escaped as \n.
- Carriage return is escaped as \r.
- Backslash is escaped as \\.
